  title="The Overtime Mistake on Millions of Shift-Work Paychecks"
  description="If you earn a night or weekend differential, your overtime rate is higher than 1.5× base — the FLSA regular rate explained with worked numbers, and how to audit your own pay stub."
  h1="The 1.5× Base Overtime Mistake"
>
  <p>
    Ask a room of shift workers what their overtime rate is and most will say "time and a half."
    Ask <em>of what</em>, and the trouble starts. If you earn a shift differential — and in 24/7
    workplaces, almost everyone does — federal law says your overtime is 1.5 times your
    <strong>regular rate</strong>, a number that includes those differentials. Payroll systems that
    multiply base rate by 1.5 are underpaying, and it happens constantly in exactly the industries
    that run rotating schedules.
  </p>

  <h2>The rule in one paragraph</h2>
  <p>
    The Fair Labor Standards Act defines the regular rate as your total pay for the workweek —
    straight-time wages, shift differentials, and most nondiscretionary bonuses — divided by the
    hours you worked. Department of Labor regulations (29 CFR part 778) state explicitly that shift
    differentials cannot be excluded. For every hour over 40 you are owed an additional one-half of
    that regular rate on top of the straight time already paid.
  </p>

  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    A plant operator earns $25 base, works 48 hours, and takes a $3/hour night differential on 24
    of those hours. Straight-time earnings: 48 × $25 + 24 × $3 = $1,272. Regular rate:
    $1,272 ÷ 48 = <strong>$26.50</strong> — not $25. The 8 overtime hours owe an extra
    8 × $13.25 = $106. The shortcut method pays 8 × $12.50 = $100. Six dollars short this week
    sounds trivial until you multiply by every 48-hour week of a rotating year — on a 2-2-3 that
    is 26 of them — and by every differential-earning coworker in the building.
  </p>

  <h2>How to audit your own stub in five minutes</h2>
  <p>
    Find a week where you worked over 40 hours <em>and</em> earned a differential. Add every
    earnings line for that week except the overtime premium itself; divide by hours worked; that is
    your regular rate. Multiply the overtime hours by half that rate and compare with the premium
    line on the stub. Or skip the arithmetic: the
    <a href="/shift-differential-calculator">shift differential &amp; overtime calculator</a> does
    the legally correct math and shows the gap versus the 1.5×-base shortcut in one screen.
  </p>

  <h2>If the numbers don't match</h2>
  <p>
    Start friendly: payroll systems are misconfigured more often than employers are malicious, and
    a single corrected setting fixes everyone's future checks. Bring one concrete week's math, not
    an accusation. If you are unionized, your steward has seen this exact issue before. The FLSA
    allows recovery of back wages (typically two years, three for willful violations), so the
    stakes rise quickly for employers who ignore a polite flag — which is precisely why the polite
    flag usually works.
  </p>

  <h2>Know what your rotation is worth</h2>
  <p>
    Differentials also change which rotation pays best. A night-heavy pattern with a strong
    differential can out-earn a day pattern at a higher base — count the night hours in your cycle
    with the <a href="/12-hour-shift-yearly-hours-calculator">yearly hours calculator</a>, price a
    nursing week with the <a href="/nurse-shift-pay-calculator">nurse pay calculator</a>, and map
    which paydays carry the fat weeks with the
    <a href="/biweekly-payday-calculator">biweekly payday calculator</a>. The schedule decides your
    life; the regular rate decides your check. Verify both.
  </p>
